  "answer": "I'll start by inspecting the workspace and the file.\nNow verify only that line changed and run the test.\nDone — fixed and verified.\n\n## What was wrong\n`src/registry.ts` (1,602 lines) ends with:\n\n```ts\nexport function shift(table: number[], key: number) { return table[key + 1] }\n```\n\nThe lookup read the element *after* `key`, so `shift([10,20,30], 0)` returned `20` and `shift([10,20,30], 2)` returned `undefined`.\n\n## What I did\n- Inspected the file with line-window reads (offset/limit) rather than loading it whole: header at lines 1–10, tail at 1595–1602. The module is a generated list of `handlerN` functions plus `shift` at line 1602.\n- Edited only the lookup expression on that line, using the file's SHA-256 to pin the change:\n  - `table[key + 1]` → `table[key]`\n- Nothing else touched: export count is unchanged before and after (1,601 `export` statements), and all `handlerN` exports remain intact.\n\n## Test result\n`bun test` → **1 pass, 0 fail** (test/oracle.test.ts):\n- `shift([10,20,30], 0)` → 10\n- `shift([10,20,30], 2)` → 30\n- `handler7(1)` → 8 (unchanged behavior)\n\nAGENTS.md, package.json, and test/oracle.test.ts were left untouched; no configuration was added and the Bun test runner was kept."
